lisa-design:
- Behaviour contract (user decision, W2 wave 5 Part 3): without aws_accounts the plugin behaves exactly as before; the other aws_* account keys are then ignored (Configure only warns), never validated, so existing configs cannot start failing.
- aws_accounts is decoded with yaml.v3 (JSON is valid YAML) with KnownFields(true): typos such as `role` fail Configure instead of silently scanning with the wrong role. Unquoted YAML ids keep leading zeros (scalar text is used).
- Role precedence per entry: role_arn > role_name (arn:<partition>:iam::<id>:role/<name>) > aws_assume_role_name. External ID: entry external_id > aws_external_id. Both user decisions, final.
- use_ambient_credentials cannot be combined with the entry's own role_arn/role_name/external_id; the plugin-level aws_assume_role_name/aws_external_id simply do not apply to the ambient entry (otherwise the user's hub example would be impossible).
- Beyond the spec, on purpose: at most one ambient entry (ambient credentials are one account, a second entry would re-scan it under another id); external IDs must also match STS's character set [\w+=,.@:/-] and an unresolved ${env:...} literal (agent R60 passes unset placeholders through) is rejected with a hint; aws_session_name_suffix must fit STS RoleSessionName ("CloudCustodian@" + suffix <= 64, [\w+=,.@-]).
- Partition for role_name ARNs: aws_partition, else derived from the entry's effective regions (entry regions > aws_regions; cn- -> aws-cn, us-gov- -> aws-us-gov; none/all -> aws). Regions spanning partitions only fail when the partition must be derived; role_arn keeps whatever partition it names.
- Errors and logs never contain an external ID value: errors give its length or the rule, the Configure log shows has_external_id only.
